   "l": "All under heaven say my Way is great,\nlike nothing at all.\nIt is just because it is great\nthat it is like nothing at all.\nIf it were like something —\nlong since! — its being petty.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Everyone under heaven says my Way is great — and that it resembles nothing. It is precisely because it is great that it resembles nothing. Had it resembled anything, it would long since have shrunk to something petty.",
   "n": "不肖 is a real word of the language, not just 'not-resembling'. 肖 is to take after one's forebears, so 不肖 is what you call a son who is a poor copy of his father: unworthy, a good-for-nothing. The line uses both senses at once — the Way is like nothing else, and the world therefore writes it off as no good. English cannot hold both. Wang Bi reads 肖 as plain resemblance, so the shipped English keeps that sense, and this note keeps the other.\n\n夫唯 X，故 Y is a fixed frame the book leans on constantly: 'it is precisely because X that Y'.\n\n久矣其細也夫 is inverted for force. The predicate 久矣 'long since!' is thrown in front of its own subject clause 其細 'its being petty', and two final particles, 也 and 夫, close it as an exclamation. Notice that 夫 opens the third line here and closes the sixth — the same character, the same reading fú, two quite different jobs.",
   "p": true
  },
