   "l": "I have three treasures;\nI hold and keep them safe.\nThe first is called tenderness,\nthe second is called frugality,\nthe third is called not daring to be first in all under heaven.\nTenderness — therefore one can be bold;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "I have three treasures, and I hold them and keep them safe. The first is tenderness; the second is frugality; the third is never daring to go in front of all under heaven. Out of tenderness one can be bold;",
   "n": "曰 is 'says', and so 'is called' — it is how this language introduces a name: 一曰慈, 'the first is called 慈'.\n\n慈 is a parent's tenderness for a child; it is the word that answers 孝 xiào, a child's duty to a parent. Not pity for the unfortunate, which is what 'compassion' would put in your head, but the fierce softness of the one who bore you. 儉 is holding back — spending little, keeping much in reserve. It is close kin to chapter 59's 嗇, and we keep the two apart in English: 嗇 'sparing', 儉 'frugality'.\n\nThe third treasure has no noun in it at all. Where 慈 and 儉 stood, there is a whole clause, 不敢為天下先, filling the same slot. Classical Chinese does not mind: anything can be a noun if it stands where a noun stands."
  },
