   "l": "not daring to be first in all under heaven —\ntherefore one can be chief of the finished vessels.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "and because one never dares to go in front of all under heaven, one can come to head everything that is made.",
   "n": "成器長 is a genuine crux, and it turns on where you cut it. Take 成 as the verb and you get 'can complete — or become — the chief of vessels'. Wang Bi cuts it the other way: his comment writes 立成器, treating 成器 as one thing, 'a finished vessel', with 長 as its chief. The English follows him, as the sole-source rule requires, and the alternative is here on the record. Taking 成器 as one word leaves the line with no verb in it at all — 'can | finished-vessel | chief' — which is why the literal says 'can be' and not 'can become'.\n\n器 is what chapter 28 meant by it: the shaped and usable things the uncarved block becomes, and by extension the officers of a state, whose chief is the ruler.\n\n長 is zhǎng here, 'chief, head', not cháng 'long' — one graph, two words, and the sense of the line is all you have to go on."
  },
